Yes, if you divide the hp being developed by a tractor by the hp-hr/gal figure, of course you will get the gal/hr. Treat the units an numbers and watch them cancel out as you do the division. Think of hp-hr/gal as a fuel efficiency indicator - how many hp you can get over an hour's time for a gallon of fuel. After all, to get the hp-hr/gal figure, they measured the hp and the gal/hr, and divided. You're just undoing their math.
However, you must use the appropriate hp-hr/gal figure. If it's hp on the drawbar, then you must use drawbar hp-hr/gal and if PTO hp, then you must use PTO hp-hr/gal. You can't use bare engine hp-hr/gal because as installed in the tractor, the engine has parasitic loads on it that the bare engine doesn't have - like AC, hydraulics, drivetrain gears spinning, fan, alternator, etc. Also, you must use the hp-hr/gal figure that corresponds to the load on the tractor because the efficiency of the tractor varies with load. For example, if the tractor is running at 50% load, then use the hp-hr/gal figure from the 50% load test, etc. Remember,…
