Colombia is at a pivotal moment on its path toward full financial inclusion: although in 2024 96.3% of adults had at least one formal financial product, cash still dominated—especially among rural and low‑income populations—and limited interoperability in the financial ecosystem constrained the growth of digital payments.

Against this backdrop, in 2022 the Banco de la República decided to design and build a new instant payment system (IPS), drawing on successful international experiences. Guided by the principle of “building on what already exists,” Bre‑B was created as an organized set of policies, rules, agreements, payment instruments, and entities, supported by a Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI) and designed to transform Colombia’s digital payments ecosystem.

 © Better Than Cash Alliance 

The main objectives of Bre‑B are to promote digital payments among individuals and small‑scale merchants to enhance competitiveness, deepen financial inclusion nationwide, reduce the use of cash, and establish full interoperability for low‑value instant payments across all financial institutions, enabling real‑time transactions 24 hours a day, 365 days a year. To ensure an inclusive process, the central bank established the Payments Systems Forum, bringing together participants from pre‑existing low‑value instant payment systems, financial regulators, banks, fintech companies, and electronic money issuers. This dialogue space facilitated knowledge exchange and fostered collaboration among ecosystem stakeholders. Throughout the process, the Better Than Cash Alliance provided technical assistance and facilitated knowledge exchange with operators of instant payment systems from countries such as Brazil (PIX), India (UPI), Ghana (GhIPSS), and Jordan (JoPACC), ensuring the incorp…

