URL: https://paymentscmi.com/insights/bre-b-impact-in-colombia/
In September 2025, Colombia joined the group of countries that operate immediate and interoperable payment systems at the national level. 1 Colombia’s system, called Bre-B, allows anyone to send and receive money in a matter of seconds, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, between accounts in different banks, cooperatives, digital wallets and fintechs.

Bre-B arose as a response to the high dependence on cash in Colombia, which is still used in 77.8% of transactions, according to Banco de la República. 2 Its objective is to modernize low-value payments, offering an immediate, secure and frictionless digital alternative. Developed in coordination with the financial sector, Bre-B seeks to facilitate transfers between entities, reduce costs, promote the formalization of the economy and expand access to financial products for all Colombians. 3 Inspired by successful cases such as Pix in Brazil and UPI in India, Bre-B is emerging as the new public infrastructure on which banks, fintechs and merchants should build their payment strategies.

In its first week of the key registration phase, prior to going live, 9.4% of the Colombian population registered at least one key on Bre-B, with more than 12 million unique identifiers activated. 4 By comparison, Pix in Brazil reached 16.5% of the population in its initial week of launch in 2020. 5 While the contexts are different, Bre-B’s strong start indicates significant initial adoption and substantial interest in immediate digital payments among Colombians.

