According to the current International system for the Classification of Diseases 11th Revision (ICD-11) diagnostic requirements, in order to receive a classification of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD), a person’s behaviour should be characterised by three essential features. First, “persistent deficits in the ability to initiate and sustain reciprocal social interaction and social communication” (World Health Organization, 2019a). Second, by “a range of restricted, repetitive, and inflexible patterns of behaviour, interests or activities that are clearly atypical or excessive for the individual’s age and sociocultural context”. Atypical responses to sensory stimuli are now included in this domain, unlike ICD-10, where unusual sensory processing was not considered a core (diagnostic) feature. Third, “symptoms should result in significant impairment in personal, family, social, educational, occupational or other important areas of functioning” and, as in previous definitions, the onset should have been during early development. Yet, some individuals with ASD can function in many contexts through exceptional…

