URL: https://exa.ai/library/publication/l9kb6x3kwfc
Does transitional justice bring stronger democracies, increase rule of law, or reduce human rights violations? Scholars and advocates of transitional justice tend to assume a positive relationship between transitional justice and improved democracies and human rights records. To date, however, no systematic analysis has explored the success of transitional justice in achieving those objectives. This paper begins to do so. Using the University of Wisconsin-Madison’s Transitional Justice Data Base, that includes over 150 countries tracked over 30 years, the paper examines the impact of specific transitional justice mechanisms (i.e., truth commissions, trials, amnesty, lustration). It explores not only whether transitional justice improves democracy and human rights, but also whether one or more mechanism does so better than other mechanisms. The paper uses measures of democracy, human rights, and rule of law found in existing data sets, including Cingranelli and Richards, Polity IV, and the World Bank’s Governance Index.
