In October 1951, more than 20 Jewish organizations met in New York and formed the Conference on Jewish Material Claims Against Germany (JCC or Claims Conference). In March 1952, the FRG held negotiations with the Government of Israel and the Claims Conference, which in September 1952 led to two agreements known as the “Luxembourg Agreements.” The agreement with Israel provided for the payment of DM 3 billion (approximately $714 million based on 1952 exchange rates) in goods and services to the State of Israel. The agreement with the Claims Conference provided for payment of DM 450 million (approximately $107 million, based on 1952 exchange rates) and obligated the FRG to enact legislation to compensate individuals.

