Why do some postconflict states return to violence, while others remain at peace? Analysts increasingly credit postconflict justice institutions-such as truth commissions, amnesties, reparations, and trials-with contributing to the prevention of conflict recurrence following civil wars. But scholars remain divided about the overall effectiveness of these varied institutions. In this article, we directly engage this debate. We seek to overcome theoretical and data limitations that make it difficult to address the relationship between justice institutions and the likelihood that conflict will recur. We find that only postconflict justice processes that lessen conflict-induced grievances, and thus address individual motivations to rebel, are successful at keeping the peace. In a series of statistical tests using new data on postconflict justice from 1946 to 2006, we find strong support for the role of motivation-addressing processes in reducing conflict recurrence. Why do some postconflict states return to violence, while others remain at peace? In June 2003, Liberian warring factions reached an agreement to end the civil war that had raged for over a decade. The Comprehensive Peace Agreement marked the last in a series of failed attempts to secure an end to the conflict. Since its signing, Liberia has experienced ten years of peace to date. While multiple factors certainly explain this success, several scholars argue that the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, implemented in Liberia shortly after the war ended, has played an important role in helping the state transition from violence to a sustainable peace. The Liberia experience is not exceptional. In addition to the well-known case of the Truth and Reconciliation Commission in South Africa, postconflict justice inst…
