To explore the gap between shifts in liberal governance and perceptions of democracy, I 
use a mixed method study: a qualitative comparative case study design and process tracing with 
quantitative elements. A mixed methods approach is the most succinct way to examine the gap 
and understanding why citizens opinions may shift. Though Hungary and Poland have both 
experienced autocratization, the approaches each incumbent took were different. Comparative 
analysis is the most effective way to study this phenomenon because it allows for a more 
systemic comparison with similarly matched control variables such as population and historical 
background (Collier 1993, 106). Singular cases do not prove cause and effect; a case comparison 
would be more effective to explain a causal mechanism (Bennet and Elman 2006, 459). There 
are three main goals of a comparative analysis: comparison for a causal analysis, examining 
cases with the idea that these concepts properly illuminate these cases, and highlighting 
differences in cases to establish how parallel processes change in different contexts (Collier 
1993, 108). 
