Unlike photo editing or the rendering engines we will get to later, video editing applications tend to be one of the most complex types of programs we test for CPUs. Premiere Pro supports a large variety of media and features, all of which take advantage of hardware differently but which are not all required for any one end user. Thus, the best CPU to use depends on the type of media and effects an end user is working with. Due to this, we break down the test into a number of sub-results, primarily by media codec.
Starting off by looking at the overall score (Chart #1) we see that the new Xeon processors outperform their last-gen counterparts by a small amount and, promisingly, appear to suffer relatively less from performance degradation at odd core counts (compare the 36-core 3475X to the 44-core 3575X). Nonetheless, they are still slower across the board than AMD’s Ryzen Threadripper PRO chips.
This relative performance stack is fairly consistent across the three sub-tests we looked at in this review, with the exception of RAW codec performance (Chart #4) where the new Xeons all manage to outperform the 7975WX. Intel has made little to no generation improvements in Premiere Pro, which is unsurprising given the limited multi-core scaling present in this application.
