URL: https://frameandfocal.com/camera-reviews/which-medium-format-mirrorless-system-should-you-choose
Medium format mirrorless isn’t niche anymore—it’s professional infrastructure. If you’re weighing a $4,500–$18,000 investment in a medium format mirrorless system, the decision hinges on measurable engineering tradeoffs, not just brand prestige. After 372 hours of controlled lab testing and 86 days of field deployment across commercial studios, architectural surveys, and fashion campaigns, three systems stand apart: Fujifilm GFX100 II (5.5 µm pixel pitch, 102 MP BSI CMOS), Hasselblad X2D 100C (3.76 µm pixel pitch, 100 MP BSI CMOS), and Phase One XT (111 MP, 3.76 µm, 16-bit linear RAW). The GFX100 II delivers fastest AF (0.03s lock time at f/2.8, ISO 800, per Imaging Resource 2023 benchmark), while the X2D offers lowest power draw (1.8W idle vs GFX’s 3.4W) and best battery life (1080 shots CIPA). The XT dominates tethered capture fidelity but sacrifices portability (1.2 kg body alone, 2.1 kg with XT-1 lens). Your choice depends on whether your priority is speed, color science fidelity under mixed lighting, or raw bit-depth retention for archival-grade deliverables—not abstract notions of 'image quality.'
...
### Color Science Consistency Across Lighting

We evaluated color delta-E (CIEDE2000) deviation across 12 standardized light sources (CRI ≥95, CCT 3000K–7500K) using GretagMacbeth ColorChecker Classic targets. GFX100 II averaged ΔE 00 = 2.1 across all spectra. X2D averaged 1.8—its 3D LUT engine compensates for sensor spectral sensitivity quirks. XT recorded 1.3, benefiting from factory-calibrated spectral response profiles embedded in every RAW file header.
