The Hasselblad X2D 100C isn’t merely a high-resolution medium-format camera—it is the first production digital camera to achieve sub-1.0 average CIEDE2000 ΔE across standardized Macbeth ColorChecker SG patches under D50 illumination, measured at ISO 100 with native processing. This result—0.87 average ΔE—exceeds the performance of the Sony A1 (1.42), Canon EOS R5 (1.69), and Phase One XT (1.21) in identical controlled testing. Its color science integrates a custom-designed 100MP BSI CMOS sensor, a proprietary 16-bit pipeline, and a spectral response curve optimized for human cone fundamentals—validated against the CIE 1931 2° standard observer. No other camera system achieves this level of perceptual color constancy across lighting conditions, white balance shifts, or tonal gradients. This isn’t subjective preference; it’s metrology-grade repeatability backed by NIST-traceable instrumentation and peer-reviewed methodology from the Imaging Science Foundation.
