[3] Tethering and 102MP Files: The Fujifilm GFX 100 II for Lookbooks - imagic Blog (2026-08-11T07:07:14.000Z)
URL: https://imagic.ink/blog/mastering-fujifilm-gfx-100-ii-for-commercial-fashion-lookbook-guide-677
Nobody shoots a commercial lookbook untethered anymore, or at least nobody should. Art directors and clients want to see selects in near real time, and stylists need to confirm garment details before you move to the next look. The GFX 100 II connects over USB-C to Capture One (Fujifilm has a dedicated GFX tethering plugin) and the transfer of a full 102MP RAW file took a consistent 1.8 to 2.4 seconds on my setup, cable and laptop dependent. That's slower than a 24MP full-frame file, which is closer to half a second, and across a 400-frame shoot day that difference adds up to real minutes of standing around waiting for the frame to populate on the tether screen.

My workaround was to shoot slightly fewer frames per setup and trust the AF more, which the improved subject tracking actually let me do. I also shot compressed RAW rather than uncompressed for anything that wasn't a hero shot, which cut transfer time by roughly a third with no visible quality loss on lookbook-scale output. If your studio's tether cable run is long, get a proper active USB-C cable rated for the distance. A cheap cable dropping connection mid-shoot can easily cost twenty minutes, which is an entirely avoidable problem.
