## The Great Divide: Frolic vs. Detour

The most common fact pattern involves an employee driving a company vehicle who decides to make a side trip. The law uses two distinct terms to categorize these side trips, and the difference is the difference between liability and dismissal.

### 1. The Detour (Liability Attaches)

A Detour is a minor deviation from the employer's designated route or business. It is regarded as a trivial departure that is "reasonably foreseeable" to the employer.

- Logic: Humans are not robots. A delivery driver will get thirsty and stop for a soda. A consultant will stop to buy a pack of cigarettes. These are incidental to the employment.
- Result: The employee is still within the scope of employment. The employer is liable.

### 2. The Frolic (Liability Cut Off)

A Frolic is a major deviation—a distinct "abandonment" of the employer's business in pursuit of the employee's own personal business.

- Logic: The employee has severed the connection to the employer. They are no longer acting as an agent; they are just a person driving a car on their own time.
- Result: The employee is outside the scope of employment. The employer is not liable.
