376 So. 3d 758 (Fla. 4th DCA 2024) · Fla. 4th DCA · January 10, 2024

Holding. The Fourth District held that, as a matter of law, a banquet manager who struck pedestrians while driving home and using his personal cell phone to call his employer was not acting within the scope of his employment, so the employer was not vicariously liable.

McKee v. Crestline Hotels & Resorts, LLC illustrates how narrowly Florida courts read the first prong of the Sussman scope-of-employment test when an off-duty employee’s commute intersects with a stray work-related task. Even where a plaintiff musters evidence that an employee was mid-call with the employer at the instant of a crash, the decision confirms that a court can resolve vicarious liability against the plaintiff as a matter of law if using a personal phone while driving was never part of what the employee was hired to do. The opinion aligns the “going and coming” rule with the key-mishandling reasoning of Allan v. Graf, treating a commuting employee’s driving — and any incidental phone use behind the wheel — as a risk of vehicle ownership rather than a risk of the employer’s enterprise. Read alongside Kulzer v. Way, which reversed summary judgment where genuine fact disputes remained about whether an employee’s mid-day stops were work-related, and Wood v. Royal Plus, Inc., which likewise rejected vicarious liability for an off-the-clock commute, McKee shows that Florida courts will affirm summary judgment for an employer whenever any one Sussman prong plainly fails, even when the other two are genuinely disputed or simply assumed in the plaintiff’s favor.
