3. Wisconsin follows the Second Restatement of Agency with respect to this question. It uses the following factors to assess scope:

(1) Conduct of a servant is within the scope of employment if, but only if: (a) it is of the kind he is employed to perform; (b) it occurs substantially within the authorized time and space limits; (c) it is actuated, at least in part, by a purpose to serve the master, and (d) if force is intentionally used by the servant against another, the use of force is not unexpectable by the master.

(2) Conduct of a servant is not within the scope of employment if it is different in kind from that authorized, far beyond the authorized time or space limits, or too little actuated by a purpose to serve the master. Olson, 156 Wis.2d at 498-499 n.10 (citing Restatement (Second) of Agency § 228 (1958)).
