Researchers at the University of Michigan have developed nanoscale Ag/a-Si:H/c-Si resistive-switching devices that significantly minimize the effect of forming by utilizing a heavily doped crystalline silicon substrate. These devices are fully CMOS compatible and show comparable scalability with enhanced performance over molecule-based devices. They exhibit rectifying I-V behavior, reducing crosstalk and enabling efficient crossbar memory and logic operations. The devices demonstrate a high on/off resistance ratio (>10^4) and robust switching, surviving over 10^5 cycles without performance degradation. Typical threshold voltages range from 3.5V to 4.5V for "on" and -3V to -4V for "off" states. Scalability has been confirmed down to 50×50 nm², with potential extension to 20 nm. These characteristics make the device structure ideal for ultra-high density memory applications and integrated logic circuits, marking a promising advance in nanotechnology-based data storage solutions.
