Resistive random-access memory (RRAM) is a futuristic technology that can be used as in-memory computing and in storage memory [1–5]. Even though this technology is promising with its nanoscale dimension as well as low power operations, the major challenge is the sneak path current originate due to low resistive paths provided by the unselected memory cells in the crossbar array (CBA) [6, 7]. To circumvent the problem of sneak path current, various techniques are employed, such as complementary resistive switching (CRS) devices [8, 9] and selector devices [10, 11]. However, it is reported that CRS is not a practical solution because of its inherent variability and the need to write back circuit [12]. To date, many selector devices such as diodes [13, 14], transistors [15, 16], and volatile bi-layer devices are reported to be used in association with RRAM. Nevertheless, the unipolar behaviour of diodes and scaling concerns of transistor switches impede them using in CBA. To overcome these shortcomings of the available selector switches, several non-linear selectors have been realised [10, 17, 18] which are fabricated either as single-layer or multi-layer material stacks with advanced engineered designs [19, 20]. The desirable characteristics of selectors such as scalability, high selectivity, high current density, low off current and compatibility with memory cell [21] enable them to use in CBA to limit leakage (sneak path) current. The selector is an essential element to be associated with conductive bridge random-access memory (CBRAM) [22–24] or RRAM device in order to reduce the sneak path current. Among the available selectors, Ta2O5/TaOx/TiO2-based selector has exceptionally good selectivity (∼104) and high current density [17]. Also, it has been proven experimenta…

