3. Fully connected MVM implementation by multiplexing. (a) How to map the last fully connected layer in the demonstration to a memristor array. All weights connected to the same output neuron are represented by two matching rows of ... ristors. These memristor cells are constructed such that every weight equals the differential conductance of a pair of memristors accordingly. The output yi is represented by the difference with regard to the accumulative currents flowing through two assortative SLs (i.e., a positive SL and the matched negative SL). (b) The practical mapping geometry for realizing the desired fully connected MVM computing. Due to the limit of 16 cells per row, the ideal 192 cells associated with the same output neuron are split into 12 rows of memristors in the fabricated array. The requisite current accumulation is therefore captured equivalently in a time division multiplexing manner by adding the 12 SL currents under their corresponding inputs. The accumulation of the SL currents occurs in the shared accumulator block. The other processing flows remain the same after obtaining the total currents. The splitting, multiplexing and accumulative processes are necessary for convolution with a large kernel, e.g., the convolutional process of C3 (see Fig. 2a).

