. The ideal nonlinear circuit elements [2, 3] have been pre-defined intentionally so that they do not mix ideal characteristics (e.g. contain both resistance and capacitance). The functional model of a physical device is a small circuit that contains more than one ideal element, and any model can be improved to better match experimental measurements by including more ideal elements in the model or refining the parameters and terms of the ideal circuit elements. In general, the more physics that is included in an ideal element model the better, as long as it is done correctly and does not alter the form of the defining equations. Now I examine the specific points from the comment. First point. Here the authors appear to be referring to table 1 on page 11 of [4]. This table is not a formal definition of a memristor, but rather guidance to those new in the field (since [4] was a Tutorial). The conditions that the resistance of a current-controlled memristor and the conductance of a voltage-controlled memristor should not diverge as the cur rent or voltage, respectively, go to zero are sufficient for constructing a proper model of an extended memristor, but perhaps not necessary, and are provided to prevent the beginner from making a mistake. I presented the formal definition for a memristor at both the very beginning and the very end of [4]. Contrary to the authors’ statement, I have never prohibited a zero resistance or zero conductance from inclusion in the definition of a memristor. However, one avoids pitfalls by using the former within a current-controlled memristor model and the latter in a voltage-controlled memristor model. To illustrate the type of contradiction that can arise for an ideal memristor that exhibits a divergence, I showed in section 9 of [4] that a…

