Holding. Florida tort law does not import workers’ compensation exceptions like the dual-purpose or bunkhouse rules, so an employee who had clocked out and was driving to his lodging when he caused an accident was not within the course and scope of employment, and his employer was not vicariously liable.

Wood v. Royal Plus, Inc. reinforces that Florida’s going and coming rule and the tort-based, three-prong Sussman test — not workers’ compensation doctrines such as the dual-purpose rule or the bunkhouse exception — govern whether an employee’s off-the-clock conduct falls within the course and scope of employment for purposes of an employer’s vicarious liability to injured third parties. The decision also confirms that constructive control over a vehicle cannot be manufactured from an attenuated chain of informal loans among an employee’s coworkers and their relatives, and it declines to create a new common law duty obligating employers to run background checks on employees who drive. Read together with Sussman and with contrasting decisions such as Kulzer v. Way, which reversed summary judgment where genuine factual disputes remained about an employee’s mid-day errands, Wood illustrates how Florida courts apply the same three-prong test to reach different results depending on whether the summary judgment record leaves any of its elements genuinely disputed.
