URL: https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/orsc.2021.1557
Nowhere is algorithmically mediated customer control more evident than in platform companies, which use digital infrastructure to connect workers with customers for short-term assignments (Lei 2021, Rahman 2021, Stark and Pais 2021). Digital labor platforms, such as Uber, TaskRabbit, and Upwork, embed customers as a foundational layer of control by using customer ratings as input for their algorithmic systems to determine workers’ visibility, eligibility for incentives, and continued employment (Leung 2014, Pallais 2014, Maffie 2020). This new configuration of control has brought startling efficiencies to platform organizations by reducing the administrative and financial burden of hiring managers to oversee workers (Aloisi 2015, Dubal 2017). However, this control system places workers in a precarious situation by granting customers control over workers with virtually zero accountability for their actions (Maffie 2020). Indeed, some scholars suggest that customers’ abuse of workers, including wage theft (O’Brien and Yurieff 2020), sexual harassment (Ticona and Mateescu 2018), and verbal attacks (Maffie 2020), is higher in platform settings than other service settings because of this lack of accountability (Ticona et al. 2018). Though researchers are beginning to highlight how workers mount collective resistance in platform settings (Chen 2018, Rizzo and Atzeni 2020, Lei 2021), we know less about how individual workers resist algorithmically mediated customer control in their day-to-day work.
