Quotas for women are among the widest reaching electoral reforms of the last thirty years. Whereas gender quotas existed in only a handful of countries in the 1970s, today more than 130 countries worldwide have modified constitutions, electoral laws, or party rules to mandate that a certain proportion of women be included as candidates or legislators. Because quotas transform the composition of parliaments, their potential impact is wide-ranging.1 Quotas have been shown to influence party strategy, legislative behavior, public opinion, political engagement, and the aspirations, education, and political efficacy of women and girls.2
