China, like other socialist regimes, has a strong legacy of state feminism being involved in promoting free marriage, maternal care, and women's participation in production and politics, especially in the Mao era. Once it became integrated into global capitalism in the reform era, China adopted an ambivalent attitude to gender inequality, where women-friendly policies, the deterioration of women's rights, and stagnant women's political participation coexist (Angeloff & Lieber, 2012; Rosen, 1995; Howell, 2002, Howell, 2006, Howell, 2008; Zhou, 2019). The absence of women in China's political leadership is also a well-documented issue. The Inter-Parliamentary Union ranked China 16th out of 193 countries in 1997 regarding the percentage of women in the national parliament and 75th in 2020.2Currently, 5.3% of the 20th Chinese Communist Party's Central Committee members are women and there was no women in the CCP's latest Politburo for the first time in the last 20years.3As the only officially sponsored organization representing women's interests in China, the All-China Women's Federation (ACWF) is often accused of being accountable for both the progression and regression in gender equality. Can today's China and its way of governing gender politics qualify as a form of “state feminism”? Is there a better way to frame and explain China's continuing gender inequality instead of merely treating it as the failure of a single organization (i.e., the ACWF)?
