The One Million Signatures Campaign to Change Discriminatory Laws was one of the important and influential experiences of the Iranian women’s movement in the 2000s; a campaign launched in September 2006 (Shahrivar 1385) with the aim of changing unequal laws against women, whose activities were based on direct dialogue with people, legal education, and a presence in cities and regions across the country. Although the campaign ultimately did not achieve its stated goal of collecting one million signatures, it succeeded in taking the issue of legal discrimination against women beyond the limited circles of civil society activists and turning it into part of the broader public conversation.

The twentieth anniversary of the campaign’s launch provided an opportunity to speak with Mansoureh Shojaee, a researcher, writer, veteran women’s movement activist, and one of the founders of the One Million Signatures Campaign, about the experience and legacy of this movement—from how the idea for the campaign took shape and how it connected with women in different parts of the country, to the diversity and internal disagreements within the women’s movement, the relationship between legal demands and the political developments of 2009 (1388), and the connection between this experience and the “Woman, Life, Freedom” movement.
