This paper focuses on the property division at the time of divorce and summarizes the historical transition of the legal provisions on the divorce property division under the Chinese Marriage Law. Looking at the Chinese marriage law historically, it can be roughly divided into three stages. The marriage law enacted in 1950, the marriage law promulgated in 1980, and the marriage law revised in 2001 (the current law). Under the current law, Article 39, Article 40, and Article 41 provide for the division of divorce property. The Supreme People's Court of China conducted judicial interpretations three times during the 10 years from 2001 to 2011 to apply to the trial. It stipulates in detail about the common property of husband and wife, personal property, and property division at the time of divorce. In addition, the Women's Rights Protection Law has been enacted to deal with women' s problems that have become apparent with the reform and opening-up policy. However, although the "Gender Equality" and "Women's Rights Protection Principles"of the current law in China are stipulated as the principles for dividing divorce property, it is difficult to realize the protection of women' s rights at a realistic level. In this study, I would like to point out that there is an urgent need for further legislation to address the issue of women's rights protection in the division of marital property. Furthermore, under the gender role, "housework", which imposes a heavy burden on women, needs to be evaluated both socially and legally. A wider legislation on women's rights protection is required.

