agricultural land) Status: 
Reported by 49 countries worldwide, including 32 countries 
from sub-Saharan Africa 

Significant gender disparities persist within the agricultural population, with women being less likely than men to own agricultural land in most countries.

Women in agricultural households are far less likely than men to own land or hold secure land rights.

In 26 of 32 countries with available data (SDG 5.a.1), a larger share of men than women own or control agricultural land.

In over 60% of countries, the gender gap exceeds 20 percentage points, reaching 42 p.p. in Mali and Benin, 53 p.p. in Côte d’Ivoire and 57 p.p. in Niger, in 2019

In Malawi, Lesotho, the Comoros, and Eswatini, a higher proportion of women than men own agricultural land.

In Burundi, Eswatini, Ethiopia, Kenya, Malawi, and Zimbabwe, women make up slightly more than 50% of landowners or rights-holders.

