INTRODUCTION 
In many countries with a history of colonialism, customary law operates 
in parallel with statutory law, with customary and religious laws governing 
family or private life.' Customary law determines personal status and 
property rights and has special implications for women's role in society and 
ability to shape their destiny. Additionally, as the United Nations 
Commission on HIV and the Law recognized, customary law impacts 
women's health and can increase HIV risk. 2 While this article focuses on 
sub-Saharan Africa, particularly Anglophone countries formerly subject to 
British colonial rule, it is broadly relevant for other countries that espouse 
customary law and have plural legal systems. 
Addressing customary law is a challenge as it is generally not officially 
recorded or uniformly applied. In addition, customary law is interpreted 
