On April 3rd, 1992, China adopted the Law on the Protection of Women’s Rights and Interests, which was amended on August 28th, 2005, October 26th, 2018, and, most recently on October 30th, 2022. As stated by the National People’s Congress, the amended Law seeks to implement a comprehensive national policy of gender equality. In particular, the Law’s amendments extend the scope of women’s personal and personality rights, including reproductive health rights. Article 21 includes the prohibition of non-medically necessary fetal gender identification and artificial termination of pregnancies motivated by gender selection, stipulating that medical institutions should respect the wishes of women when performing relevant medical activities. Article 30 sets the standards for women’s health service systems, requiring the establishment of mental health support services, comprehensive healthcare coverage over the full reproductive cycle, maternal and child health care institutions, regular health examinations for female employees, and reasonably equipped public facilities for women's needs Regarding employment and social security measures affecting reproductive health rights, Article 51 clarifies the obligations employers owe to their female employees. It further requires employers not to restrict female employees from promotion, evaluation, appointment, and awarding of professional and technical titles or positions because of marriage, pregnancy, maternity leave, and/or breastfeeding. In addition, the amended Law sets out various supporting measures for implementing rights protection in practice. For example, Article 28 requires that media reports on events involving women shall be objective, appropriate, and proportionate, and must not infringe on women’s personality rights.

