South Africa’s agricultural sector was exposed to heavy government intervention in the period leading up to the first democratic elections in 1994, mainly in the form of statutory market interventions and direct and indirect support to producers. These measures were removed in a process that started in the 1980s, but that accelerated after the elections and was completed by the late 1990s. This process of deregulation and liberalisation resulted in a sector that was exposed to global market forces for the first time in decades.

