[5] Evaluating the implementation and outcomes of the Global Compensation Deed in Zimbabwe by Partson Paradza, Ronald Mpofu, Joseph  A. Yacim, Benita Zulch :: SSRN (2025-12-24T20:45:25.000Z)
URL: https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5962710
This study examines the status of the 2020 Global Compensation Deed (GCD) signed between the government of Zimbabwe (GoZ) and former commercial farmers (FCFs) to resolve the longstanding compensation dispute. The GCD was designed to guide the processes and procedures for paying the agreed compensation to farmers for improvements made to their expropriated land. However, despite being in the final year of the GCD's lifespan, no study has comprehensively assessed its implementation and effectiveness. Thus, this research sought to investigate the status of the GCD. Employing a descriptive design, the study utilised archival data and conducted semi-structured interviews with targeted stakeholders. Findings indicate that the government has struggled to fulfil its financial obligations, leading to payment delays and the consideration of alternative proposals involving Treasury Bonds. These proposals have been met with mixed responses from the farmers, with some expressing dissatisfaction regarding the interest rates and the extended payment periods. The study identified several key challenges, including the government's failure to meet its payment obligations under the GCD, uncertainties about funding sources, and disagreements among some FCF representatives regarding the future direction of the compensation process. The study emphasises the critical need for transparency, accountability, and equitable treatment of all stakeholders to achieve successful, just dispute resolution.

