The majority of commentary on Zimbabwe's land reform claims that farm labour has all been displaced, agricultural production has almost completely collapsed, food poverty is rampant, rural economies are in grave danger, and political "cronies" have acquired control of the land. This essay contends that the tale is far more nuanced and complex, with both accomplishments and failures, and that it contains more than just collapse and tragedy. Some of the most important conclusions from a two-decade desk research of Zimbabwe's land reform are summarised in the report. The report describes the nature of the agrarian structure's profound transformation and its effects on agricultural production and lifestyles. A review of who received the land reveals the variety of recent settlers, many of whom have made significant financial investments in their new farms. Middle farmers, an emerging segment that produces, invests, and accumulates, are identified. Future economic and political repercussions of this are significant, as is discussed in the concluding section on policy problems. 

