 October 2025 
 
 
ZIMBABWE 
 Between 2011 and 2017, extreme poverty—calculated using the national food poverty line—rose from 23 to 30 percent of the population. It 
 increased further to 38 percent in April-May of 2019. Welfare inequality, as measured by the Gini index, also rose from 43 in 2011/12 to 45 in 
 2017 and 50 in April-May of 2019. Imputation using household phone surveys conducted after the pandemic suggests a further increase in the 
 extreme poverty rate, reaching 49 percent at its peak in mid-2020 and decreasing to 43 percent in 2021 on the backs of record harvests. Thus, 
 the number of poor increased from 4.5 million in 2017 to 7 million in 2020. The latest estimate shows poverty to be 42 percent in 2022. The 
 proximate causes for the increase in extreme poverty during 2011-2022 were macroeconomic volatility, recurrent droughts, inflation, and 
 economic contraction related to COVID-19, exacerbated by extreme weather events like Cyclone Idai.  
 
