Revitalizing Zimbabwe’s Agricultural Economy after Fast Track Land Reform:

The Urgency of Enhanced Livelihoods

1Charles Chavunduka, 2Magreth Mahuni, 3Patience Mazanhi

1University of Zimbabwe,

ABSTRACT

cmchavunduka@yahoo.com,  Harare,
Zimbabwe

2University of Zimbabwe,

magrethmahuni@gmail.com,  Harare,
Zimbabwe

3Center for Development Research
(ZEF),

Mazanhip24@gmail.com, Bonn,
Germany

Context and background

Colonisation  of  Zimbabwe  was  accompanied  by  the  displacement  of
indigenous  people  from  rich  agricultural  land  and  their  relocation  to
marginal areas of production. The Lancaster House Agreement of 1980 was
the  first  official  effort  to  return  white-owned  farm  land  to  indigenous
Zimbabweans.  Resistance  by  white  commercial  farmers,  and  political
bickering,  slowed  meaningful  progress.  Early  in  2000,  war  veterans  and
assorted political allies began random invasions of white farms, many farm
families escaped and most left Zimbabwe for good. This wave of invasions,
sanctioned by the state, came to be known as the Fast Track Land Reform
Programme.  A  large  amount  of  white-owned  land  was  returned  to  black
families, but much of the land has remained vacant and underutilised. The
social and economic costs of this situation are unsustainable.

Goal and Objectives:

The paper aims to offer a way forward concerning the revitalization of the
agricultural sector, and thus of the economy of Zimbabwe.

Methodology:

The  research  methodology  uses  a  case  study  design,  and  data  collection
consists of literature and documentary review complemented by empirical
data from statistical reports.

Results:

The  results  highlight  the  need  for  active  state  support  beyond  land
redistribution  and  control.  Needed  is  deliberate,  inclusive  government
support  and  collaboration  with  reform  beneficiaries  for  enhanced
livelihoods.

Keywords

Land, Agriculture, Livelihoods, Resettlement, Economy, Zimbabwe

