1. INTRODUCTION

Agriculture remains the backbone of Zimbabwe’s economy, employing over 60% of the population
and  contributing  to  food  security,  rural  incomes  and  national  development  (Ministry  of  Foreign
Affairs  and  International  Trade,  2021),  and  contributing  17%  to  GDP  (The  World  Bank,  2024a).
However,  since  the  implementation  of  the  Fast  Track  Land  Reform  Programme  (FTLRP)  in  2000,
Zimbabwe’s  agricultural  sector  has  undergone  dramatic  structural  changes  accompanied  by  food
insecurity.  The  FTLRP  aimed  at  addressing  colonial  land  ownership  imbalances  by  redistributing
land  from  white  commercial  farmers  to  landless  blacks  (Moyo,  2011).  While  the  programme
corrected colonial injustices, it led to new inequalities based on power, and significant collapse in
large-scale agricultural output.

Following  the  FTLRP,  large  scale  commercial  horticulture  production  declined  sharply due  to  the
displacement of established commercial farmers, many of whom had long standing export contracts
and access to capital and inputs (Moyo, 2011). Zimbabwe lost a significant share of its horticulture
export  markets  in  Europe  due  to  reduced  output,  lack  of  compliance  with  quality  standards  and
disruption in supply chains (Richardson, 2005). Since the early 2010s, efforts by the government,
non-governmental  organisations,  and  donor  agencies  (through  the  Zimbabwe  Agenda  for
Sustainable  Socio-Economic  Transformation  -  ZimASSET  and  National  Development  Strategy  1  -
NDS1)  supported  horticulture  revival  through  input  support,  contract  farming,  and  irrigation
rehabilitation (IFAD, 2022). Citrus exports to the Middle East and Asia saw modest recovery with 57
000  tonnes  exported  in  2022  (ZimStat,  2025).  Private  sector  led  contract  farming  especially  for
macadamia nuts, blueberries and chilies has grown post 2015 and blueberry exports increased to
over USD18 million by 2022 (ZimTrade, 2022). The area under blueberries increased by 3% from
650 hectares in 2023/2024 season to 670 hectares in 2024/2025 season (ZimStat, 2025).

Despite  signs  of  recovery  in  key  subsectors  such  as  tobacco  production,  which  rose  from  48  000
tonnes in 2008 to over 260 000 tonnes in 2023 due to contract farming, production of key staple
foods, that is maize and wheat, remains volatile and climate sensitive (TIMB, 2023). Maize production
declined from 2.1 million tonnes in 1999/2000 to below 500 000 tonnes by 2007/2008 and peaked
again from 907 000 tonnes in 2016/1017 to 2.7 million tonnes in 2020/2021 due to government
support  through  Pfumvudza  and  Command  Agriculture  (Government  of  Zimbabwe,  2021).  Maize
self-sufficiency peaked at 93% in 2022 but significantly declined to 27% in 2024 (ZimStat, 2025).
Wheat production dropped significantly after 2000 due to the collapse of irrigation infrastructure
but average production rose from 20 000 to 60 000 tonnes (2000-2008), 60 000-120 000 tonnes
(2009-2015) and 375 000 tonnes in 2022 (FAO, 2023). Wheat shows gradual improvement in self-
sufficiency from 54% in 2021, 66% in 2022 and 68% in 2023 but dropped to 62% in 2024 (ZimStat,
2025).  Nonetheless,  Zimbabwe  experienced  recurring  food  insecurity  with  over  5  million  people
considered food insecure due to droughts and economic instability as of 2020 (WFP, 2021). Access
to inputs, credit, irrigation infrastructure, and agricultural  extension services remains uneven and
politised, limiting productivity for the majority of smallholder farmers (ZEPARU, 2021). Weak land
tenure arrangements, particularly the absence of tradable title deeds or secure 99-year leases further
inhibit investment and long-term planning (Chimhowu, 2006).  Only  6% of resettled farmers  hold

African Journal on Land Policy and Geospatial Sciences ISSN:2657-2664, Vol.9 Issue7 (September 2026)
1259

AJLP&GS, e-ISSN: 2657-2664, Vol.9 Issue 7 https://doi.org/10.48346/IMIST.PRSM/ajlp-gs.v9i7.62428

formal  99-year  leases  and  this  lack  of  land  titles  limits  collateral  use  for  bank  loans  thereby
restricting private investment in agriculture (The World Bank, 2024b).

Critically, many scholars argue that the agricultural sector must be redesigned to enhance livelihoods
which remain precarious due to food insecurity, vulnerability to shocks, and poverty (Chavunduka
et  al.,  2021;  Scoones,  2015;  Chideme,  2021;  Moyo,  2021).  Recurrent  droughts,  coupled  with
unaffordable  agricultural  inputs,  have  deepened  land  underutilisation  (FAO,  2021).  Government
initiatives  such  as  the  Presidential  inputs  scheme,  Command  Agriculture  and  Pfumvudza  were
introduced to boost agricultural land utilisation but with mixed results (Shonhe, 2018). Command
agriculture is a government-run contract farming scheme launched in 2016 to boost food security by
increasing the production of staple crops like maize. It provides selected farmers with production
inputs  such  as  seeds,  fertilizer,  and  chemicals,  and  they  are  required  to  sell  their  produce  to  the
government’s Grain Marketing Board (GMB).

The cost of the inputs is then deducted from the payment for the delivered crops. Pfumvudza is a
government programme that promotes conservation agriculture to improve food security, especially
for vulnerable households. It uses techniques like digging small pits, using mulch, crop rotation, and
minimum  tillage  on  small  plots  to  increase  crop  yields  and  conserve  water  and  soil  fertility.  The
programme aims to make farming more resilient to climate change, empower smallholder farmers
and  boost  national  food  reserves.  This  study  explores  the  urgent  need  to  revitalise  Zimbabwe’s
agricultural  economy  in  a  manner  that  links  agrarian  growth  with  livelihood  enhancement,  rural
enterprise development, and climate resilient systems. It answers the question: How can Zimbabwe’s
agricultural  economy  be  revitalised  following  fast  track  land  reform?  In  addressing  the  question
about how Zimbabwe’s agricultural economy can be revitalised post FTLRP, the study assesses the
outcomes  of  post  land  reform  agricultural  interventions  and  it  examines  policy  and  institutional
pathways towards inclusive and sustainable recovery.

In  the  next  section  we  present  the  literature  review.  The  section  is  followed  by  the  research
methodology. The findings of the study are presented and discussed with a focus on improving the
policy  environment,  climate  resilient  agriculture  and  rural  enterprise  development.  Lastly,  some
conclusions are drawn and recommendations for future research made.

2. LITERATURE REVIEW

The colonial regime dispossessed indigenous Africans of land, a process that created enduring socio-
economic inequalities based on race (Moyana, 1984; Moyo, 2011). In Zimbabwe, land grabbing dates
back to the time of colonisation in 1890, and force was used in land expropriation (Cheater, 1990).
By 1919, all land in Zimbabwe was owned by be the government except that given out as free grants
to members of the  Pioneer Column. The  Land Apportionment Act of 1930, institutionalised racial
segregation in land ownership by reserving over 51% of arable land for about 50 000 white settlers,
while  millions  of  blacks  were  confined  to  overcrowded  and  less  fertile  native  reserves  (Palmer,
1977). The Native Land Husbandry Act of 1951, among other measures such as limiting grazing in
African reserves, removed Blacks who remained in White areas (Arrighi, 1966; Rukuni and Eicher,
1994).

African Journal on Land Policy and Geospatial Sciences ISSN:2657-2664, Vol.9 Issue7 (September 2026)
1260

AJLP&GS, e-ISSN: 2657-2664, Vol.9 Issue 7 https://doi.org/10.48346/IMIST.PRSM/ajlp-gs.v9i7.62428

At independence in 1980, Zimbabwe adopted a willing buyer willing seller land reform model due to
the Lancaster House Agreement (1979) conditions. The Lancaster House Agreement protected white
farmers’  property  rights  for  10  years  post-independence,  thereby  delaying  compulsory  land
redistribution  (Raftopoulos  and  Mlambo,  2009).  About  3.5  million  hectares  of  land  were
redistributed  to  71  000  households  through  market-based  mechanisms  between  1980  and  1990
(Kinsey,  1999).  The  first  decade  of  land  reform  was  limited  in  scope,  with  underutilisation  of
redistributed land and failure to alleviate rural poverty (Moyo, 1995). In the late 1980s and 1990s,
amidst  advice  by  the  International  Monetary  Fund  (IMF)  to  liberalise  the  economy,  Zimbabwe
adopted  the  Economic  Structural  Adjustment  Programme  (ESAP)  in  1991.  The  failure  of  ESAP
resulted in de-industrialisation, massive retrenchment of labour, and increased demand for land as
a source of livelihood.

In 1992 the government enacted a Land Acquisition Act which allowed compulsory acquisition of
land while only compensating white farmers for improvements made to the land (Moyo, 2000). The
Zimbabwe  government  demanded  Britain,  as  the  former  colonial  master,  to  provide  money  for
compensating farmers who were going to lose land through the compulsory land acquisition process.
But  in  1997,  the  newly  elected  Labour  Government  in  Britain  “denied  that  it  had  any  historic
responsibility  for  land  expropriation  on  the  grounds  that  its  members  were  not  the  land-owning
stock” (Moyo, 2001, 317).  The  British government’s stance  -  followed by the  1999  formation  of a
labour-based political party in Zimbabwe that posed a formidable electoral challenge to the ruling
party paved the way to a radical land reform in the form of the FTLRP.

Zimbabwe  launched  the  FTLRP  in  2000,  which  involved  the  compulsory  acquisition  of  about  11
million hectares of white owned land and its redistribution to approximately 160 000 households in
an  often  chaotic  and  politically  charged  manner  (Moyo,  2011).  Under  the  FTLRP,  government
facilitated  the  speedy  settlement  of  people  on  farms,  while  the  provision  of  infrastructure  and
services were to be taken care of in the future as and when resources became available. This is yet to
come, but a 2025 policy allows beneficiaries of land acquired under the FTLRP to apply for title as a
way of securing their land tenure. While the FTLRP succeeded in dismantling the colonial agrarian
structure  and  broadening  land  ownership,  it  also  triggered  widespread  disruption  in  production,
investment  and  agricultural  support  systems.  Food  insecurity  remained  high  especially  between
2002  and  2009  as  many  households  lacked  capacity,  resources  and  support  services  to  become
productive on agricultural land (FAO, 2021).

2.1 Theoretical Framework

Theories explain how things are related and why they are the way they are or the rationale behind
particular phenomena and prescribe solutions to problems. The point of any theory is to allow one
to  see  things  one  would  not  be  able  to  see  otherwise  (Graeber,  2007).  Scholars  have  developed
various theories and models to explain how the agricultural sector can be managed and improved.
Some  of  the  theories  are  relevant  in  finding  solutions  to  solve  challenges  facing  the  agriculture
economy after the FTLRP in Zimbabwe. This study is informed by the developmental state theory,
the sustainable livelihoods framework, and cluster model.

