2.1.1 Developmental State Theory

Developmental state theory refers to a model in which the government plays a central and proactive
role  in  guiding  economic  development  especially  industrialisation  through  strategic  planning,
investment and coordination with the private sector (Leftwich,  1995). The concept emerged from
the study of East Asian “Tiger Economies” which achieved rapid industrialisation between the 1960s
and 1990s through strong state involvement in economic planning (Wade, 1990). Building on Evans’s
(1995) idea of embedded autonomy, a developmental state is characterised by a strong, capable state
apparatus that is autonomous from narrow elite interests but embedded in society (Kanyenze et al.,
2017).  A  developmental  state  maintains  a  competent,  professional  and  insulated  bureaucracy
capable  of  designing  and  implementing  industrial  policy,  while  remaining  connected  to  societal
needs through institutions and consultations. Developmental states implement targeted subsidies,
invest  in  key  industries,  protect  infant  industries,  and  invest  in  education  and  infrastructure  to
promote growth (Leftwich, 1995).

Revitalising the agricultural economy after the FTLRP demands strategic planning and coordination
- especially in land utilisation, infrastructure development, and input supply. The state has a crucial
role in directing economic growth through the delivery of extension services, overseeing land audits,
transparent input distribution, public investment in human capital and building inclusive and pro-
poor  livelihoods.  Developmental  state  theory  promotes  structural  transformation  from  low
productivity  subsistence  farming  to  modern  commercial  agriculture  (Mkandawire,  2001).  The
building  of  physical  infrastructure  such  as  roads,  irrigation  schemes,  storage  facilities,  and  rural
electrification  is  vital  for  modernising  agriculture  and  reducing  transaction  costs  for  smallholder
farmers  (FAO,  2010).  However,  in  Zimbabwe  centralisation  of  power  has  led  to  corruption,
patronage,  and  politicisation  of  agriculture  thereby  weakening  the  developmental  state  theory’s
potential  (Moyo,  2021).  Fiscal  capacity  of  the  state  -  and  strong  institutions  and  policy
implementation must be taken into account as they are major constraints (Kanyenze et al., 2017).
Zimbabwe needs sustainable land audits, crop yield data systems, and accountability structures to
guide and improve reform outcomes (ZimStat, 2020).

2.1.2 The sustainable livelihoods framework

The sustainable livelihood idea was first introduced by the Brundtland commission on Environment
and Development as  a  way of linking socio-economic and ecological  considerations in a  cohesive,
policy relevant structure. The UN Conference of 1992 (Agenda 21) advocated for the achievement of
sustainable livelihoods as a broad goal for poverty reduction (Adato and Meinzen-Dick, 2002). The
sustainable  livelihoods  framework  (SLF)  was  promoted  by  the  UK  Department  of  International
Development  (DFID)  in  the  late  1990s  drawing  on  earlier  work  by  Robert  Chambers  and  Gordon
Conway (DFID, 1999).

The Sustainable Livelihoods Framework (Figure 1) refers to an analytical tool used to understand
how people access and use various assets (human, natural, financial, physical and social capital) to
achieve  livelihood  outcomes  within  a  context  shaped  by  vulnerabilities,  institutions  and  policies
(DFID,  1999).  The  vulnerability  context  considers  the  shocks,  trends  and  seasonality  that  affect
people’s livelihoods such as drought, economic instability or disease outbreaks which are outside the

African Journal on Land Policy and Geospatial Sciences ISSN:2657-2664, Vol.9 Issue7 (September 2026)
