The SLF emphasises agency and local level strategies and informs pro-poor and inclusive agricultural
policy.  It  provides  a  holistic  view  of  rural  livelihoods  explaining  why  land  alone  is  not  enough  to
secure
livelihoods  (Scoones,  1998).  However,  the  SLF  assumes  rational  beings,  thereby
underestimating politics and is  weak in addressing macroeconomic and institutional challenges  –
gaps that can be filled in by a developmental state (De Haan, 2012; Dorward et al., 2003).

2.1.3 Cluster Model

The  Cluster  Model  describes  clustering  as  the  interconnectedness  between  related  businesses,
suppliers and associated institutions that are geographically or territorially adjacent or operating in
the same field targeting the same objective (Saidov and Ochilov, 2023; Porter, 1998). Clusters are an
efficient  and more  practical way for  regional economic  analysis, determining which cluster needs
more support and improvement. In agrarian terms, clustering brings together farmers who could be
in product or geographic proximity and this has the potential of creating agglomerations that in turn
lead  to  economies  of  scale,  thus  expanding  agricultural  productivity  (Steffens,  2016).  Tanzania
presents  a  good  example  of  successful  clustering  that  led  to  conglomeration  of  537  smallholder
farmers  for  an  Irish  Potato  project  (Ibid).  This  was  supported  by  intensive  research  and  inputs
provision as  farmers  made  collective  efforts  and coordination, supported by the  SAGCOT Pvt  Ltd.
Successful  clusters  require  geographic  proximity,  trust  building,  and  access  to  information  for
empowerment  of  smallholder  farmers  (Galvez-Nogales,  2010;  Vasylieva,  2016).  The  benefits  of

