agricultural  clustering  include  efficiency,  innovation,  cooperation  and  increasing  competitiveness.
However, it has been observed that the absence of clustering in Zimbabwe has been a hindrance in
the progression of small-scale agriculture (Munyawarara and Govender, 2020).

The  three  theories,  that  is,  the  developmental  state,  sustainable  livelihoods  framework  and  the
cluster model combine to provide a framework for building enhanced livelihoods. The state provides
policies, the SLF agency and local level strategies and clustering enhances networking, innovation
and relations of trust among farmers.

Clustering  particularly  of  informal,  small  and  medium  scale  farmers  is  a  key  strategy  to  enhance
agricultural  productivity,  thus  enhancing  agricultural  industrialisation  and  empowerment  (ACET,
2015).  However,  Munyawarara  and  Govender  (2020)  reveal  that  the  absence  of  clustering  in
Zimbabwe has been a hindrance in the progression of small-scale agriculture. IFAD (2025) reports
the  initiatives  carried  out  by  Government  of  Zimbabwe  and  the  Ministry  of  Lands,  Agriculture,
Fisheries, Water and Rural Development (MLAFWRD), with the support of IFAD’s USD35.50 million
loan support for Smallholder Agriculture Cluster Projects (SACP). It indicates project anomalies in
that intended cluster project beneficiaries have not yet fully accessed the support despite receiving
at least 70% of the loan amount. A lot of intended beneficiaries are said to still lack access to water
to support agricultural activities. The effort by the country to reclassify Agricultural Innovation Zones
(AIZs) to enhance food security, is one sure effort to build on clustering. According to Tsiko (2020),
AEZs  involve  land  that  has  similar  ecological  characteristics  that  include  climate  and  soil.  This
provides a good classification of farmers in clusters and thus build on common strengths that will
enhance productivity and competitiveness of smallholder farmers. Over the years, the three theories
have  been applied in one  way or the  other in developing countries including Zimbabwe, but with
failure to address the development problem. In the context of the FTLRP it is not understood why the
state has not capacitated beneficiaries of the land redistribution programme. Could it be out of lack
of resources? Could it be a lack of strategy? This is the knowledge gap that the research addresses.

3. RESEARCH METHODOLOGY

The  study uses a qualitative approach based on  case  study design to explore  the  outcomes of the
FTLRP and pathways to revitalisation of the agricultural economy. A case study research design is a
research  strategy  that  is  based  on  in-depth  analysis  of  a  case  or  an  investigation  of  a  specific
phenomenon  within  its  real-life  context  (Cresswell,  2014).  The  study  uses  Zimbabwe  as  the  case
where the FTLRP took place. The data presented in the study was obtained from a desktop literature
and  documentary  review.  Literature  and  documentary  review  refer  to  a  systematic  analysis  of
existing academic studies, government reports, policy documents, media articles and organisational
publications relevant  to the  research topic (Hart, 1998).  The  research question seeks to establish
how Zimbabwe’s agricultural economy can be revitalised post fast track land reform? Documentary
review  of  policy  statements,  land  reform  reports,  agricultural  strategies  and  statistics  is  done  to
provide  an  understanding  of  the  agricultural  economy  post  fast  track  land  reform.  The  approach
allows for comparative assessment of agricultural productivity, food security, and livelihoods after
the FTLRP. The data analysis process involved organising it into thematic folders. Emerging themes
were then analysed in line with the research question, allowing for comprehensive thematic content

