In 2024, the global labor market has definitively shifted toward services: services account for 50.32% of total global employment, industry for 23.60%, and agriculture for 26.07%. Since the beginning of the century, the share of agricultural employment has declined by 13.27 percentage points, industrial employment has increased by 2.24 p.p., while services have gained 11.03 p.p. This shift reflects urbanization, rising productivity in agriculture, and the expansion of digital, financial, and tourism-related services. The structural sequence “agriculture → industry → services” is becoming the norm even in middle-income economies.
