Since the early 2000s, major economies have undergone pronounced structural shifts. In China, the share of agricultural employment fell from 50.04% to 22.22% (−27.82 p.p.), while industry rose to 31.63% (+6.99 p.p.) and services to 46.15% (+20.84 p.p.). In India, agriculture declined from 59.61% to 42.35% (−17.25 p.p.), industry increased to 25.37% (+9.03 p.p.), and services to 32.27% (+8.22 p.p.); similar de-agrarianization is observed in Indonesia (−17.39 p.p. in agriculture) and Nigeria (−18.17 p.p.). In Latin America, Brazil has strengthened its service specialization (services at 71.98%; +9.69 p.p.) alongside a shrinking industrial share, while in Europe Russia increased its services share to 68.80% (+12.31 p.p.). In advanced economies such as the United States, the trend is more gradual: services at 79.39% (+4.45 p.p.) alongside declining industrial employment.

