To overcome the lack of complete interaction data, we designed and developed a custom audio recorder to capture and log user interactions with a commercial VA—Amazon’s “Alexa”—as audio files. We recorded each interaction from start to finish, plus an additional 10 seconds post-interaction to document users’ verbal and vocal responses during and immediately after their interactions. We designed the audio recorder to be compact, packageable and easily deployable, also evidenced by our recent work [Mahmood et al., 2025b]. We deployed Amazon Echo Dot smart speakers augmented with our recording device at 15 older adults’ homes for 4 weeks to capture rich, “in-the-wild” interaction data. While prior work has explored error types and recovery strategies for both older adults [Angelini et al., 2021; Cuadra et al., 2022; Kim and Choudhury, 2021; Pradhan et al., 2019; 2020; Stigall et al., 2019] and general users [Ashktorab et al., 2019; Beneteau et al., 2019; Myers et al., 2018], our study presents new insights into older adults’ interactions with VAs and highlights design considerations for making VAs more accessible to this special population: (1) most errors arise from VAs’ limited understanding capabilities and inaccuracies in speech capture, highlighting the need for improved comprehension of diverse speech styles; (2) temporal error trends reveal that query reformulation and learning by experience alone are insufficient for effective error management for older adults and there is need to account for their age-related challenges such as forgetfulness; (3) leveraging users’ immediate reactions as implicit cues and increasing the use of VA-initiated recovery can effectively identify and manage interaction breakdowns.
