Abstract.
Supporting older adults in health self-management is crucial for promoting independent aging, particularly given the growing strain on healthcare systems. While voice assistants (VAs) hold the potential to support aging in place, they often lack tailored assistance and present usability challenges. We addressed these issues through a five-stage design process with older adults to develop a personal health assistant.
Starting with in-home interviews (N=17N=17), we identified two primary challenges in older adult’s health self-management: health awareness and medical adherence.
To address these challenges, we developed a high-fidelity LLM-powered VA prototype to debrief doctor’s visit notes and generate tailored medication reminders. We refined our prototype with feedback from co-design workshops (N=10N=10) and validated its usability through in-home studies (N=5N=5). Our work highlights key design features for personal health assistants and provides broader insights into desirable VA characteristics, including personalization, adapting to user context, and respect for user autonomy.
Keywords: voice assistant, LLMs, voice interactions, ChatGPT, conversational assistants, self-management, health, aging adults, co-design
1. Introduction
Aging gracefully is everyone’s desire yet chronic health conditions often stand in the way. Nearly 95% of the older adult population suffers from at least one chronic ailment,
and 80% endure two or more (National Council on Aging, 2022).
Managing multiple chronic conditions is a formidable task for the aging population (Schoenberg et al., 2009; Upshur and Tracy, 2008), especially for those who live in social isolation and lack a support system—a reality for 31% of women and 19% of men aged 65 and older (on Aging-Related Statistics, 2020).
Cognitive impairment, affecting one in five older adults (Pais et al., 2020), further hinders their ability to independently follow health management routines by impairing their understanding, memory, decision-making, and the ability to follow instructions.
These challenges are made even more pressing by rapidly growing aging population—by 2030, one in six people globally will be over 60, and the number is expected to double by 2050 (World Health Organization, 2022). This demographic shift is straining caregivers and healthcare systems, which are increasingly overwhelmed by the demands of caring for older adults (Bardach et al., 2011; Giovannetti et al., 2012; Shahly et al., 2013; Mitchell and Walker, 2020).
In this landscape, promoting self-care and self-management is crucial for older adults’ well-being and for supporting aging in place (Davies, 2011; McLeroy et al., 1988; Garnett et al., 2018).
The importance of self-management is underscored by WHO’s introduction of mAgeing (mobile aging), which aims to help older adults maintain functional ability and live as independently and healthily as possible through evidence-based self-management and self-care interventions at a national level (World Health Organization, 2018).
These interventions can empower individuals to take charge of their health, promoting independence and better overall health (Helbostad et al., 2017). While mobile health (mHealth) applications offer promise for supporting self-management, they often face significant usability issues. Many mHealth applications are overly complex and not designed with the needs and preferences of older adults in mind (Wildenbos et al., 2019).
Voice assistants (VAs) are a promising alternative to support autonomy and improve quality of life (Smith et al., 2023; Blair and Abdullah, 2019) as older adults prefer speech-based over text-based interfaces (Kowalski et al., 2019; Wulf et al., 2014).
Despite VAs’ potential to serve as health aids (Shade et al., 2020; Bolaños et al., 2020; Brewer et al., 2022; Harrington et al., 2022; Sanders and Martin-Hammond, 2019; Chen et al., 2021), they are also not designed with older adults in mind (Stigall et al., 2019). Current VA technologies have two main gaps that limit their ability to effectively support self-management:
•
Usability issues: older adults find it difficult to create and manage event-specific reminders, such as appointment and medication reminders, with voice assistants (Mahmood et al., 2024; Shade et al., 2020; Bolaños et al., 2020).
•
Lack of user health context: currently, no commercial VA (e.g., Alexa, Siri) offers personalized medical assistance; these VAs can provide general Q&A about health but lack information tailored to the users’ specific health conditions.
To address these issues in VAs for health self-management, we conducted a five-stage design study with older adults. We started with in-home interviews (N=17N=17) to learn more about older adults’ perspectives on health management, identifying two primary challenges: staying aware of their health status and adhering to health regimens. Based on these insights, we designed and developed a high-fidelity prototype VA by integrating a large language model (GPT-4) into a commercial VA (Alexa), focusing on three key phases: 1) processing patient’s after-visit summary to understand their medical context; 2) debriefing user on the information from the after-visit summary to enhance awareness; and 3) creating tailored medication reminders to improve adherence. To further refine the prototype, we held three co-design workshops (N=10N=10) where participants provided actionable considerations based on their interaction with the initial prototype. We used the insights gathered to refine the VA prototype. Finally, we evaluated the usability and effectiveness of this refined VA using older adults’ personal health data (N=5N=5), finding a high mean usability score of 85 as measured by the System Usability Scale (SUS) (Brooke et al., 1996), with all participants successfully navigating the after-visit debrief (phase 2) and medication reminder creation (phase 3). Our work has the following contributions:
•
From in-home interviews, we identified challenges and breakdowns the aging population faces in self-management of chronic diseases (Section 3).
•
Through a multi-stage design process, we developed an LLM-powered personal health assistant to address awareness and adherence challenges to support older adults in self-management (Section 4, 5, and 6).
•
Through an in-home validation study, we found empirical evidence demonstrating the effectiveness and usability of our personal health assistant in debriefing the after-visit summary, handling personal health-related queries and setting personalized reminders (Section 7).
2. Related Work
There are various ways to support older adults in health self-management to facilitate aging in place. In this work, we focus on voice assistants and review prior research on their roles in supporting older adults’ health management.
2.1. Current Voice Assistants to Support Older Adults’ Health
Voice user interfaces (VUIs) show promise in supporting older adults by offering a natural, hands-free way to interact with digital information (Kumah-Crystal et al., 2018). Speech input is three times faster and has a 20% lower error rate than typing (Ruan et al., 2016), which is especially beneficial for older adults as they may have physical or technological limitations. Hence, older adults prefer speech-based over text-based interfaces (Kowalski et al., 2019; Wulf et al., 2014). VAs have shown significant potential in various aspects of health self-management, offering the potential to enhance older adults’ quality of life and autonomy (Smith et al., 2023; Blair and Abdullah, 2019). They assist in medical information seeking (Brewer et al., 2022; Harrington et al., 2022; Sanders and Martin-Hammond, 2019) and setting reminders for management of pain and other medical conditions (Shade et al., 2020; Bolaños et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2021; Bolaños et al., 2020; Jesús-Azabal et al., 2020). Despite their potential in healthcare applications, commercial VAs are not specifically designed for older adults (Stigall et al., 2019), leading to two major challenges that hinder their effectiveness: 1) usability and 2) lack of user context.
2.1.1. Usability challenges
Voice assistants are marketed and designed to mimic human-like conversations (Harris, 2004; Gilmartin et al., 2017). Therefore, users may treat VAs as if they are engaging in human dialogue (Cowan et al., 2017; Doyle et al., 2019a), specifically older adults (Mahmood et al., 2024). The inaccurate user mental model of VA capabilities emerging from the notion of humanlike interactions results in users overestimating the VAs’ conversational abilities, leading to communication breakdowns when the technology falls short of these expectations (Luger and Sellen, 2016; Leahu et al., 2013; Moore et al., 2016). Such misconceptions have been shown to impact older adults’ health-related queries (Mahmood et al., 2024; Brewer et al., 2022). For instance, a detailed analysis of health information-seeking behaviors
has shown that such human-like expectations result in various conversational breakdowns e.g., VA not being able to answer or giving a wrong off-topic answer to user queries (Brewer et al., 2022). These usability challenges are exacerbated for marginalized populations e.g., people with cultural differences (Harrington et al., 2018) and physical disabilities (Pradhan et al., 2018).
Usability challenges are also prevalent in available functional features such as setting reminders.
Although commercial VAs offer reminder setting functionality, it is sub-optimal (Mahmood et al., 2024). For instance, older adults often struggle to set daily reminders for medications, resulting in the need to set a new reminder each morning (Mahmood et al., 2024), which defeats the purpose as they still have to rely on their memory every day.
Thus, having users set VA reminders themselves (Mahmood et al., 2024; Shade et al., 2020; Bolaños et al., 2020) leaves considerable room for error that can be detrimental to crucial tasks such as remembering to go to doctors’ appointments or taking medications.
While reminder features exist, they are not intuitive or clearly tailored for specific tasks, leaving them wanting a dedicated medication reminder function (Chen et al., 2021).
A voice assistant that automatically creates reminders for older adults could be highly beneficial. For example, the Snips VA provides reminders for medications and doctor’s appointments based on health information, but it requires medical professionals and care providers to manually input this data (Jesús-Azabal et al., 2020). This process not only increases the workload for care providers but also makes older adults dependent on their healthcare providers for yet another task, which may be neither desirable nor practical. Thus, no current VA supports tailored reminder creation for medication and appointment self-management.
2.1.2. Lack of user context
The lack of user context in interactions leads to more usability issues, particularly with current commercial voice assistants that treat each user query as a standalone event (Beirl et al., 2019; Kim and Choudhury, 2021; Liao et al., 2018). These VAs do not account for even basic conversation history, which could serve as a minimalistic proxy for user context. Implementing multi-turn conversations with large language models (LLMs) has been shown to enable more robust and fluid interactions with users (Mahmood et al., 2023; Chan et al., 2023; Mahmood et al., 2024), especially in health information-seeking scenarios, and helps reduce conversational breakdowns. However, this level of context may still fall short of fully understanding and supporting the needs and expectations of older adults from VA to support self-management. To the best of our knowledge, no VA currently incorporates personal health data when responding to health information queries or when helping users set events and reminders. In this work, we explore how we may design a VA that integrates user context—including conversation history, the user’s daily routine, and the user’s personal health information—to effectively support older adults in health self-management.
2.2. Older Adults’ Vision for Voice Assistants in Health Self-Management
Older adults have expressed clear expectations for voice assistants in managing their health and well-being, particularly in the context of self-care and self-management.
Older adults believe that VAs could offer a more comprehensive overview of their health, allowing them to access a “holistic health” summary that includes updates on their health progress (Martin-Hammond et al., 2019; Pradhan et al., 2020; Harrington et al., 2018). Their expectations for VAs extend beyond basic know-how about their health to include preventive care, with a desire for VAs to play a proactive role in monitoring health indicators and offering timely advice (Yamada et al., 2018). For instance, older adults have noted that they would appreciate being “addressed by the smart speaker” proactively and having the VA implement a “watch-over” function to ensure their well-being (Yamada et al., 2018).
VAs are also envisioned as tools that can simplify and enhance daily routines and health management tasks. Older adults emphasize the importance of VAs being easy to set up, intuitive to use, and robust enough to avoid frequent breakdowns (Chen et al., 2021). They expect VAs to handle periodic health management tasks with minimal user effort, highlighting the importance of maintaining and utilizing conversation history and personal data shared by the user to reduce their workload over time (Chen et al., 2021). Additionally, older adults value the ability to control and easily modify reminder settings, emphasizing the need for flexibility and personalization in these systems (Takagi et al., 2018). They also express a strong desire for control and agency in managing their health data and making informed decisions, along with increased transparency about how their data is being used (Sanders and Martin-Hammond, 2019; Chen et al., 2021).
These expectations of older adults, as articulated through interviews and observational studies, provide valuable insights into their envisioned VA for self-management. However, these insights often lack focus on practical aspects of design and implementation.
Therefore, in this work, we involved older adults as co-designers to create a voice assistant that meets their healthcare needs. We developed a working prototype of an LLM-powered VA to support older adults’ self-management by using their personal health data (after-visit summary) and preferences to provide tailored assistance with after-visit debriefs and medication reminder creation.
Research Overview
We involved older adults in a multi-stage design process (see Fig. 2) to develop
a voice assistant to support self-management. The following sections detail the design process, which includes five stages:
1) in-home interviews with older adults to identify breakdowns and challenges in their current health management practices (Section 3),
in-lab development of an initial voice assistant prototype to bring back to older adults for the co-design workshops (Section 4),
3) co-design workshops with older adults to gather design considerations for the VA (Section 5),
4) refining the initial prototype VA based on co-design findings (Section 6), and
5) an in-home validation study to assess the effectiveness and usability of the refined VA interaction (Section 7). All activities with older adults were approved by our institutional review board and all participants were compensated at a rate of 15$/hr.
