Voice assistants offer potential for aiding older adults with mobile app navigation, yet understanding their intentions remains challenging. To address this, we explore using large language models to enhance intention comprehension in voice assistants for older adults. Qualitative research on Chinese older adults reveals five categories of expression failures: localised expressions, colloquialisms, multitasking, incomplete expressions, and ambiguous expressions. We propose tailored methods for each, employing prompts to translate original instructions into formal ones and interpret free text instructions into structured apps and operations. Experiments with 44 tasks reflecting real interaction failures validate our approach's effectiveness. By tackling intention understanding failure, our work improves voice assistant usability for older adults, contributing to AI technologies' inclusive advancement.
